type Chunk = { text: string; section: string; start: number; end: number };

export function chunkDocument(doc: string, maxChars: number): Chunk[] {
  const chunks: Chunk[] = [];
  const paragraphs = doc.split("\n\n");
  let text = "";
  let start = 0;

  for (const paragraph of paragraphs) {
    if (text && text.length + paragraph.length + 2 > maxChars) {
      chunks.push({ text, section: "unknown", start, end: text.length });
      start += text.length;
      text = "";
    }
    text += (text ? "\n\n" : "") + paragraph;
  }

  if (text) {
    chunks.push({ text, section: "unknown", start, end: start + text.length });
  }

  return chunks;
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
**Prediction.** Take a page that reads: heading `Refunds`, paragraph `Refunds are accepted within 30 days of delivery.`, then a list item `Enterprise plans: up to 60 days, subject to the contract schedule.` The packer appends paragraphs until the character budget is about to overflow, so whether the list travels with the sentence it excepts depends on how long the earlier paragraphs happened to be. When the list lands in the next chunk, retrieval returns a passage that is confidently about refunds and silent about the exception, and the model answers "30 days" for an enterprise customer: grounded in the retrieved text and wrong in the world. That is the 0.94 / 0.55 split — the document is found, the passage needed to answer is not, and the model has no way to know something is missing.

QA's complaint has a different cause: the offsets. `start` advances by `text.length` but never by the two-character separator, so every `start` after the first boundary is short by two characters per boundary; the mid-loop `end` is `text.length` — the chunk's own length — instead of `start + text.length`, so only the first chunk (where start is 0) has bounds that look right. The UI slices `raw.slice(start, end)` and highlights the wrong sentence, drifting further the deeper the chunk sits in the document. Provenance that is subtly wrong is worse than none: reviewers learn to distrust every citation.

**Defects, in impact order.**

1. **Structure is discarded.** `section` is the literal string `"unknown"`. The heading that scopes a passage ("Refunds", "Enterprise plans") never reaches the chunk, so `up to 60 days` arrives with no idea what it qualifies, and section filters are impossible.
2. **Boundaries cross meaning and there is no overlap.** Splitting on paragraphs is better than a fixed window, but a fact that needs its neighbour is still cut, and nothing gives the tail of a document a minimum size — the last chunk can be a dozen characters that retrieves on nothing and burns a slot.
3. **Oversize blocks are not handled.** The budget is checked *before* adding a paragraph, never on the paragraph itself, so a table or long list pasted as one paragraph is emitted whole: it dilutes similarity, eats prompt budget, and hides every row behind one vector.
4. **Offsets are broken** as above, and no `docId` / `docVersion` is stored, so a nightly re-index cannot address the previous chunk set of a document: edited paragraphs and deleted pages keep answering under stale ids.
5. **No boundary test exists.** Nothing in the pipeline asks a question whose evidence spans two chunks, so the failure mode that is hurting faithfulness is invisible to the eval.

**Refactor.**

```ts
type Chunk = {
  docId: string; docVersion: string; section: string;
  text: string; start: number; end: number;
};

type Block = { kind: "heading" | "para" | "row"; text: string; start: number };

export function chunkDocument(
  docId: string, docVersion: string, raw: string, maxChars = 1200
): Chunk[] {
  const chunks: Chunk[] = [];
  let section = "untitled";
  let buffer: Block[] = [];
  let size = 0;

  const flush = () => {
    if (buffer.length === 0) return;
    const first = buffer[0];
    const last = buffer[buffer.length - 1];
    chunks.push({
      docId, docVersion, section,
      text: buffer.map((b) => b.text).join("\n\n"),
      start: first.start,
      end: last.start + last.text.length,
    });
    // carry the last block as modest overlap: a fact split across the boundary stays whole in one chunk
    buffer = buffer.slice(-1);
    size = buffer.reduce((n, b) => n + b.text.length, 0);
  };

  for (const block of parseBlocks(raw)) {        // headings, paragraphs, list items, table rows, with true offsets
    if (block.kind === "heading") { flush(); section = block.text; continue; }
    if (block.text.length > maxChars) {          // a pasted table or an endless list
      flush();
      for (const piece of splitOnRows(block, maxChars)) {
        chunks.push({ docId, docVersion, section, ...piece });
      }
      continue;
    }
    if (size + block.text.length + 2 > maxChars) flush();
    buffer.push(block);
    size += block.text.length + 2;
  }
  flush();
  return chunks.filter((c) => c.text.trim().length > 0);
}
```

The shape matters more than the helpers: `parseBlocks` returns real offsets so `start` / `end` come from the source text instead of being accumulated, the section is carried down from the last heading, oversize blocks are split on rows or sentences rather than dropped into one chunk, and only a tail block is repeated as overlap so a condition and its exception can co-occur. For chapters that need document-level context, index a section or document summary alongside the passages, the way RAPTOR layers recursive summaries over leaves (https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.18059).

**Verification, which is the part people skip.**

1. Assert the round trip over the whole corpus: for every chunk, `raw.slice(c.start, c.end) === c.text`. Offset drift fails loudly here instead of becoming a wrong highlight in the UI.
2. Add the boundary eval: questions whose evidence spans adjacent chunks, compared against the old chunker on recall@k and answer faithfulness, plus chunk-length distribution to catch 12-character tails and 8,000-character tables.
3. Re-index with chunk ids derived from `docId` + `docVersion`, so the nightly run deletes the previous set and an edited paragraph actually disappears.
